Action: select_top_k  
Action Input: {"k": 3, "metric": "rmse"}

Thought: The ranking is unstable and many models are highly correlated; pruning redundant models should give a cleaner pool, then inverse‑error weighting (with a bit of shrinkage to reduce variance) may beat the plain mean on pool1.  
Action: prune_redundant  
Action Input: {"pool":"pool_full","corr_threshold":0.95,"metric":"rmse"}

Thought: The baseline mean on pool1 is best so far; a robust median on the same small, stable pool might improve robustness to the unstable ranking.  
Action: evaluate_strategy  
Action Input: {"combine":"median","pool":"pool1","rationale":"robust median on the stable 5‑model pool"}

Thought: The ranking is unstable and errors are highly correlated, so a weighted average that shrinks toward uniform may improve over plain mean while avoiding overfitting.  
Action: weights_inverse_error  
Action Input: {"pool":"pool1","shrinkage":0.2}

Thought: try inverse‑error weights on the pruned 11‑model pool without shrinkage to let the best models dominate.  
Action: weights_inverse_error  
Action Input: {"pool":"pool5","shrinkage":0.0}

Thought: Test weighted inverse_error on pool1 with moderate shrinkage – might improve over plain mean while staying robust.  
Action: weights_inverse_error  
Action Input: {"pool":"pool1","shrinkage":0.2}

Thought: Test the weighted inverse-error combination on the stable pool1 and on the pruned pool5, then try a slightly shrunk version on pool5 to see if it improves over the current leader mean pool1.

Action: evaluate_strategy
Action Input: {"combine":"weighted","pool":"pool1","weights":"w2","rationale":"inverse-error weighted on the stable 5‑model pool"}

Thought: Create a new inverse-error weight handle on pool1 with no shrinkage to see if full inverse-error weighting improves over the current best weighted pool1 w2.  
Action: weights_inverse_error  
Action Input: {"pool":"pool1","shrinkage":0.0}

Thought: Evaluate the current weighted scheme with no shrinkage (w4) and test a more aggressive shrinkage (0.5) and a dataset‑level meta‑model weighting to see if we can beat the best score.  

Action: evaluate_strategy  
Action Input: {"combine":"weighted","pool":"pool1","weights":"w4","rationale":"test raw inverse‑error weights on stable pool"}  

Thought: Create a new inverse‑error weight handle with higher shrinkage to reduce variance.  

Action: weights_inverse_error  
Action Input: {"pool":"pool1","shrinkage":0.5}  

Thought: Evaluate the high‑shrinkage weighted scheme.  

Action: evaluate_strategy  
Action Input: {"combine":"weighted","pool":"pool1","weights":"w5","rationale":"test inverse‑error with 0.5 shrinkage"}  

Thought: Generate a meta‑model weight handle using cross‑series smape.  

Action: weights_meta_model  
Action Input: {"pool":"pool1","scope":"dataset","metric":"smape","eta":1.0}  

Thought: Evaluate the meta‑model weighted scheme.  

Action: evaluate_strategy  
Action Input: {"combine":"weighted","pool":"pool1","weights":"w_meta","rationale":"test dataset‑level meta‑model weights"}

Thought: The current best uses raw inverse‑error weights on a 5‑model pool that still contains many correlated errors. Pruning redundant models from this pool should reduce noise and improve the weighted combination. I’ll start by pruning pool1 and then evaluate the weighted raw strategy on the pruned pool.

Action: prune_redundant
Action Input: {"pool":"pool1","corr_threshold":0.95,"metric":"rmse"}

Thought: The current best uses raw inverse‑error weights on the stable 5‑model pool.  I’ll test the same on the pruned pool6 (4 models) and a slightly shrunk variant, then see if a trimmed mean on pool6 gives a robust alternative.  
Action: weights_inverse_error  
Action Input: {"pool":"pool6","shrinkage":0.0}  
Thought: Create raw inverse‑error weights for pool6.  
Action: evaluate_strategy  
Action Input: {"combine":"weighted","pool":"pool6","weights":"w5","rationale":"raw inverse‑error on pruned pool6"}  
Thought: Now test a mildly shrunk weight set on pool6.  
Action: weights_inverse_error  
Action Input: {"pool":"pool6","shrinkage":0.2}  
Thought: Evaluate the shrunk weights.  
Action: evaluate_strategy  
Action Input: {"combine":"weighted","pool":"pool6","weights":"w6","rationale":"shrinked inverse‑error on pool6"}  
Thought: Finally try a trimmed mean on the same pruned pool to get a robust baseline.  
Action: evaluate_strategy  
Action Input: {"combine":"trimmed_mean","pool":"pool6","trim_pct":0.2,"rationale":"trimmed mean on pruned pool6"}

Thought: Evaluate the raw inverse‑error weights on the pruned pool6 to confirm its performance before exploring other pools.  
Action: evaluate_strategy  
Action Input: {"combine":"weighted","pool":"pool6","weights":"w5","rationale":"evaluate raw inverse-error weights on pruned pool6"}

Thought: Test weighted per horizon on pool6 to see if the best model changes across horizon.  
Action: weights_best_per_horizon  
Action Input: {"pool":"pool6","metric":"rmse"}

Thought: evaluate the best‑per‑horizon weighted combination on the pruned pool6 to see if per‑horizon specialization improves over the raw inverse‑error weights.

Action: evaluate_strategy

Action Input: {"combine":"weighted","pool":"pool6","weights":"w6","rationale":"test best_per_horizon weights on pruned pool"}

Thought: The weighted combination on the pruned, stable pool6 with inverse‑error weights (w5) gives the lowest validation score and matches the robust, trend‑dominated regime suggested by the diagnostics.  
Action: accept  
Action Input: {}

